# Deep dives

[deep:highspot-agent-workflow] The agent workflow system for Highspot's platform team
Problem: Before the agent workflow system, developers on the team simply wrote custom prompts every time they worked with an AI agent. There was no workflow framework or system, and no spec-driven development or anything like it.
What Peter did: Peter designed the workflow, wrote its agents and skills, built its tooling in TypeScript on Bun, and introduced it to the team. Because the approach was so new to the team, he introduced it in a standalone session for the whole team, then ran one-on-one sessions with each developer who started using it in their development workflow.
How it worked: The agents and skills were based on a forked and enhanced version of the open-source ai-agents project (rjmurillo/ai-agents). It provided a library of specialist agents (analyst, architect, implementer, QA, security, critic and others) and skills, plus an orchestrator agent that routed work to the right agents automatically. Developers were moved through the right parts of the workflow for the specific needs of what they were working on. Its quality gates, such as critic reviews and QA passes, meant the team did not take agents at their word: an agent's work was independently validated before it was accepted and the work moved on.

Peter did not use ai-agents' own memory solution. For the workflow's memory he used Basic Memory, run locally. Basic Memory stores knowledge as plain Markdown files; each file is a node, and its observations and relations together form a knowledge graph. The memory files lived in the codebase. On startup, Basic Memory ran locally for each developer and provided semantic search over that knowledge through MCP, alongside the code; reading and writing the Markdown files also went through MCP. This let the team work on a project over many sessions and rehydrate each session's context in a targeted way through MCP search.

The enhanced agents and skills, together with an MCP wrapper around the Basic Memory MCP, were packaged as a Claude plugin that unified the tooling and made it easy to use. Specs became dependency-mapped plans in which plans and tasks both carried dependencies, so up to four developers and their agents could see which work was unblocked, claimed, or safe to run in parallel.
Result: The team used the system to build two major components: the streaming Markdown component and the data table, which was defined by 21 specs and 189 requirement documents. It supported up to four developers and their agents, and in Peter's words it "could easily support more". Several developers and their agents could work on one feature at the same time without collisions. Specs, plans and code came out as consistent, reviewable artifacts, and long features kept their context across many sessions through the shared memory. The team's velocity and quality went up. The system also shaped Peter's later work, including his brain plugin.
Tools: TypeScript and Bun; a Claude plugin; a forked and enhanced version of the open-source ai-agents project (rjmurillo/ai-agents) for agents, skills and the orchestrator; Basic Memory (Markdown files, a knowledge graph, semantic search) for memory; and MCP, including a wrapper around the Basic Memory MCP.
Not public: The source code of the team's ai-agents fork, and the plugin's code and configuration. What the team's specs, plans and memory files said about Highspot's work. The names of colleagues and Highspot's internal team structure.

[deep:highspot-ai-standards] The AI development standards Peter introduced at Highspot
Problem: The engineers had been given a mandate to lean into AI agents, but no framework or direction for doing it. Many did not know how to use AI deeply and powerfully in development, so they still wrote much of their code by hand, or used agents only minimally. In Peter's view, giving developers tools to use agents well is critical for a mandate like that; the polar-ui MCP served the same purpose.

AI coding agents also started each session without the project's context, and every developer's agent setup differed. Agents looked everything up from scratch every time, because research and analysis from earlier work were not kept anywhere they could find.
What Peter did: Peter introduced many of the team's AI development standards. He wrote the repository's agent context files (CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md), checked shared skills and agents into the repository for everyone, and made specs the starting point for agent work. He also taught the standards to the team and reviewed their adoption.
How it worked: The standards were spec-driven development, shared skills and agents checked into the repository, agent context files (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md), and rules that keep agents on Polar UI components. Shared artifacts, such as analysis and research, were persisted into the project and made available through semantic search over MCP. Agents searched those memories instead of looking everything up from scratch every time, and the knowledge built up over time.
Result: Agents gave consistent results for every developer on the team. They used fewer tokens, because they searched the shared memories instead of starting over; for a component as large as the data table, which does a great many things, Peter calls this "really powerful". Developers began using their specs to have agents create the Jira epics, stories and tasks, which made backlog planning much easier. The team could communicate, plan and deliver more work, more reliably.
Tools: Claude Code and Cursor, with CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, shared skills, agents and rules; Basic Memory's MCP for semantic search over the shared analysis and research; and Jira, where agents created epics, stories and tasks from specs.
Not public: What Highspot's CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, skills and rules actually say. The names of colleagues and Highspot's internal team structure.

[deep:highspot-mcp-server] The polar-ui MCP server, the design system's contract for AI coding agents
Problem: Before the polar-ui MCP server, AI coding agents at Highspot often sampled the Highspot app at random and copied the legacy UI components they found there, instead of using the Polar UI design system. Sometimes they did not know Polar UI existed. When agents did use Polar UI, they guessed props, variants and patterns that do not exist. Agents could read designs through the Figma MCP but could not map them to the right Polar UI components. Complex components, such as the data table, were assembled inconsistently, with poor architecture and an inconsistent user experience.
What Peter did: Peter led the organization-wide effort. He designed and built the server, and he defined what it exposes to agents. He made sure the official Figma MCP, provided by Figma, worked together with the polar-ui MCP: agents picked up designs through the Figma MCP, then used the polar-ui MCP to build them. He gave the organization-wide introduction to the polar-ui MCP, showing how it worked and why the team built it, and he demoed it to the executive team. He also wrote the Confluence documentation that explained to every team how to install and use it.
How it worked: The server gave agents each component's API (props, variants and types), usage rules, and working examples and composition patterns. It lived in the Polar UI codebase. It was built on an enhanced version of Polar UI's public API export, which was generated from the components' TypeScript types and doc comments. Because of that, every new component was included in the MCP automatically, and the MCP was always fully up to date. Peter also wrote the rules that went into the application's AGENTS.md, telling its agents how to use the polar-ui MCP.
Result: The polar-ui MCP became a standard part of every Cursor and Claude Code setup for front-end work at Highspot, used by about 100 people across all application teams, design and product. Agent-written UI used Polar UI components instead of legacy ones, building screens from Figma designs got faster, and code reviews needed fewer design-system fixes. Application developers moved much faster: agents built full Figma designs accurately with Polar UI components, so developers could focus on their application-specific logic. Product owners built much more accurate prototypes and answered Polar UI questions for themselves and their teams. The Polar UI platform team also used the MCP when it built new design-system components on top of existing ones.
Tools: TypeScript with the official Model Context Protocol TypeScript SDK, Bun, and Polar UI's generated public API export. The MCP worked alongside the official Figma MCP, in Cursor and Claude Code.
Not public: The server's source code, its internal tool names and its configuration. Highspot's application code, its legacy components and its internal repositories. The names of colleagues and executives, and Highspot's internal team structure.
